      "path": "cordis.patch.yml",
      "text": "# dsh-qr-phone-login bundle layer.\n#\n# `publicOrigin` is the address a phone uses to reach this DSH. It lives here\n# rather than in plugin code because only the operator knows which name their\n# reverse proxy serves: Tailscale Serve, for example, publishes\n# `https://<machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net` onto 127.0.0.1:3080, and that exact name\n# is what the QR code must carry.\n#\n# The name is not interchangeable: the launch token binds the resulting cookie\n# to the request authority. A QR code pointing at 127.0.0.1 would mint a cookie\n# for 127.0.0.1, which is useless on a phone. With no `publicOrigin` the card\n# renders an explanation instead of a code.\n#\n# Replace the placeholder below with your own origin, or override this row from\n# your profile's own cordis.patch.yml:\n#\n#   - id: qr-phone-login\n#     config:\n#       publicOrigin: 'https://your-host.example.ts.net'\n#\n# The token itself appears in no configuration and in no file: the Host reads it\n# per request through `ctx.connection.authenticatedUrl()`.\n- insert:\n    - id: qr-phone-login\n      name: 'dsh-qr-phone-login'\n      config:\n        # Placeholder: set this to the name your phone opens.\n        publicOrigin: ''\n"
    }
  ],
